# The one thing this package cannot do for you, said at the moment it
# becomes relevant. NVIDIA's neural renderer runtime is not redistributable,
# so no repository can ship it, and without it the program starts and the
# picture is never processed - which is a worse failure than not starting.

post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'

NeuralScreen needs two things this package cannot provide:

 1. NVIDIA's neural renderer runtime. Put your copy of nvngx_dlssnr.so at

        ~/.local/share/neuralscreen/nvngx_dlssnr.so

    (or point NS_NR_DLL at it). It is not redistributable, which is why it
    is not in here.

 2. A Wayland session on an RTX 50 card. NGX refuses the feature on
    anything below Blackwell; the menu's GPU dot goes green only when the
    worker really created it.

Run it with:  neuralscreen
Diagnostics:  neuralscreen --diag     (then attach NeuralScreen.log)

The first launch asks once for the screen to capture and once for the
hotkeys - that is the Wayland security model, and the answers are
remembered.

Notes for your desktop:
  GNOME      the overlay is an ordinary window (no layer-shell in Mutter),
             and the tray icon needs the AppIndicator extension
  wlroots    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr ships no global shortcuts; join the
             'input' group for the direct fallback

/usr/share/doc/neuralscreen/README.md has the rest.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    post_install
}
